.PP
For v1 protocol errors, \f[B]channel\f[R] is \f[CR]None\f[R] /
\f[CR]null\f[R] / \f[CR]\-1\f[R] since v1 has no channel concept.
.SH C error message retrieval
The C binding provides a function to retrieve a human\-readable
description of the last error:
.IP
.EX
const char *bc_error(bc_client_t *client);
.EE
.PP
This returns a pointer to a thread\-local string buffer containing the
error message from the most recent failed operation.
The string is valid until the next API call on the same client handle.
Returns \f[CR]\(dqno error\(dq\f[R] if the last operation succeeded.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
int rc = bc_eval(client, \(dqexit 1\(dq, &result);
\f[B]if\f[R] (rc < 0) {
    fprintf(stderr, \(dqeval failed: %s (code %d)\(rsn\(dq,
            bc_error(client), rc);
}
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SH Python
.IP
.EX
\f[B]from\f[R] bashclient \f[B]import\f[R] BashClient, AuthError, TimeoutError, ServerError

\f[B]try\f[R]:
    client = BashClient.connect(timeout=5.0)
    result = client.eval(\(dqecho hello\(dq, timeout=10.0)
    print(result.stdout)
\f[B]except\f[R] AuthError \f[B]as\f[R] e:
    print(f\(dqAuthentication failed: {e}\(dq)
\f[B]except\f[R] TimeoutError \f[B]as\f[R] e:
    print(f\(dqOperation timed out: {e}\(dq)
\f[B]except\f[R] ServerError \f[B]as\f[R] e:
    print(f\(dqServer error on channel {e.channel}: {e}\(dq)
\f[B]finally\f[R]:
    client.close()
.EE
.SH TypeScript
.IP
.EX
\f[B]import\f[R] { BashClient, AuthError, TimeoutError, ServerError } \f[B]from\f[R] \(aq\(atbashclient/core\(aq;

\f[B]try\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] client = \f[B]await\f[R] BashClient.connect({ timeout: 5000 });
    \f[B]const\f[R] result = \f[B]await\f[R] client.eval(\(aqecho hello\(aq, { timeout: 10000 });
    console.log(result.stdout);
} \f[B]catch\f[R] (e) {
    \f[B]if\f[R] (e \f[B]instanceof\f[R] AuthError) {
        console.error(\(gaAuthentication failed: ${e.message}\(ga);
    } \f[B]else\f[R] \f[B]if\f[R] (e \f[B]instanceof\f[R] TimeoutError) {
        console.error(\(gaOperation timed out: ${e.message}\(ga);
    } \f[B]else\f[R] \f[B]if\f[R] (e \f[B]instanceof\f[R] ServerError) {
        console.error(\(gaServer error on channel ${e.channel}: ${e.message}\(ga);
    }
} \f[B]finally\f[R] {
    \f[B]await\f[R] client.close();
}
.EE
.SH C
.IP
.EX
#include \f[B]<bashclient.h>\f[R]
#include \f[B]<stdio.h>\f[R]

int main(void) {
    bc_client_t *client = NULL;
    bc_result_t result = {0};
    int rc;

    rc = bc_connect(&client, NULL, 5000);
    \f[B]if\f[R] (rc == BC_ERR_TRANSPORT) {
        fprintf(stderr, \(dqCannot connect: %s\(rsn\(dq, bc_error(client));
        \f[B]return\f[R] 1;
    }
    \f[B]if\f[R] (rc == BC_ERR_AUTH) {
        fprintf(stderr, \(dqAuth failed: %s\(rsn\(dq, bc_error(client));
        bc_close(client);
        \f[B]return\f[R] 1;
    }

    rc = bc_eval(client, \(dqecho hello\(dq, &result);
    \f[B]if\f[R] (rc == BC_ERR_TIMEOUT) {
        fprintf(stderr, \(dqEval timed out\(rsn\(dq);
    } \f[B]else\f[R] \f[B]if\f[R] (rc == BC_ERR_SERVER) {
        fprintf(stderr, \(dqServer error: %s\(rsn\(dq, bc_error(client));
    } \f[B]else\f[R] \f[B]if\f[R] (rc == 0) {
        printf(\(dq%s\(dq, result.stdout);
    }

    bc_result_free(&result);
    bc_close(client);
    \f[B]return\f[R] 0;
}
.EE
.SH Java
.IP
.EX
\f[B]import\f[R] \f[B]com\f[R].\f[B]bashserver\f[R].\f[B]client\f[R].*;

\f[B]public\f[R] \f[B]class\f[R] Example {
    \f[B]public\f[R] static void main(String[] args) {
        \f[B]try\f[R] (BashClient client = BashClient.connect(
                BashClient.options().timeout(5000))) {
            EvalResult result = client.eval(\(dqecho hello\(dq,
                    EvalOptions.builder().timeout(10000).build());
            System.out.println(result.getStdout());
        } \f[B]catch\f[R] (AuthException e) {
            System.err.println(\(dqAuth failed: \(dq + e.getMessage());
        } \f[B]catch\f[R] (TimeoutException e) {
            System.err.println(\(dqTimed out: \(dq + e.getMessage());
        } \f[B]catch\f[R] (ServerException e) {
            System.err.printf(\(dqServer error on channel %d: %s%n\(dq,
                    e.getChannel(), e.getMessage());
        } \f[B]catch\f[R] (BashClientException e) {
            System.err.println(\(dqClient error: \(dq + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
.EE
.SH NOTES
The C binding has two additional error codes (\f[CR]BC_ERR_NOMEM\f[R]
and \f[CR]BC_ERR_PARAM\f[R]) that do not appear in the other languages.
These cover failure modes that the managed\-language runtimes handle
through their own exception hierarchies (Python \f[CR]MemoryError\f[R] /
\f[CR]TypeError\f[R], TypeScript compile\-time checks, Java
\f[CR]OutOfMemoryError\f[R] / \f[CR]IllegalArgumentException\f[R]).
.PP
All error classes in Python, TypeScript, and Java carry a
\f[B]message\f[R] attribute (accessed via \f[CR]str(e)\f[R],
\f[CR]e.message\f[R], or \f[CR]e.getMessage()\f[R] respectively) with a
human\-readable description.
The C equivalent is the \f[CR]bc_error()\f[R] function.
.PP
Error codes in C are guaranteed to be negative integers.
Success is always indicated by \f[CR]0\f[R].
Positive return values are reserved for future use as informational
status codes.
.PP
When catching errors, prefer catching the most specific subclass first.
In Java, note that \f[CR]BashClientException\f[R] extends
\f[CR]RuntimeException\f[R] (unchecked) \(em it is not required to
declare it in \f[CR]throws\f[R] clauses, but doing so is recommended for
documentation purposes.
